 One scroll behaviour for every scrolling chat log on the site: `chat()`'s log and `ux/Dictate/Widget.js`'s `Thread` (the Dictate page and the ✦ sheet build their chat through that class, not `chat()`, since it owns a lighter box with no selection/reaction/drill wiring). The third user is Chat v2: `ext/drawer/ChatView.js` calls it on the v2 sheet with a `MutationObserver` as `observe`, so v2 and the bubble chat scroll, lock and show the ↓ button the same way (bottom-right, `align-self: flex-end` so a flex-column box keeps it on the right). `Thread` used to hand-roll its own broken copy; CLAUDE.md law 6 says fix it once, here. `box` is the element that actually scrolls (`overflow-y: auto`); a jump-to-bottom button is appended into it, so `box`'s CSS also needs `position: relative` for the button to anchor to it.
 
+**The ↓ button is dim while you read above, and lit once something new arrives below** (`has-new`, 2026-11-06, asked for /live/): a new element added to the box while unlocked lights it; the button's own move to the end does not count. Jumping or scrolling back down clears it. /live/ uses the same function on its scroll area (`public/live/LivePage.js`, `follow()`).
+
 `locked` starts true: the reader is at the bottom, so every new line follows (the owner: sticks to the bottom, follows every new message, unless scrolled up, in which case it stays put and a small button appears; clicking it jumps down and resumes following). Scrolling up turns it false and shows the button; reaching the bottom again (by hand or the button) turns it on.
 
 `pin()`, called once after drawing new lines, jumps to the bottom only while locked. A `ResizeObserver` on `box` also calls it, so a composer growing under the log, or the box's first layout (0px tall until the page is on screen), re-pins with no scroll event. See `doc/scroll.md` for why only a move up unlocks (reading "am I at the bottom?" on every event lost the lock on first load, because our own jump-to-bottom's scroll event arrives a frame later, after the box is already taller). The button is moved to the very end of `box` every jump, never left as its first child, so `.chatbox-log > :first-child`'s push-to-the-bottom margin (Chat.css) still lands on the real first message.
